#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Julia is a dynamic programming language designed primarily for numerical and"
" scientific computing. Its high-performance numerical computing capabilities"
" make it a natural fit for quantum simulation workflows. In this tutorial, "
"we show how Julia is used for both classical pre- and post-processing (for "
"example, building Hamiltonians, running ODE (ordinary differential equation)"
" solvers, and computing expectation values) and for orchestrating quantum "
"hardware jobs, eliminating the need to switch between languages or "
"environments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To interface with IBM Quantum&reg; hardware from Julia, this tutorial uses "
"two packages from the Qiskit ecosystem: `Qiskit.jl` wraps the Qiskit C "
"library and provides circuit construction and transpilation functionality in"
" Julia; `QiskitIBMRuntime.jl` connects to IBM Quantum hardware through the "
"IBM Quantum Compute Service client, enabling job submission and result "
"retrieval directly from Julia.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this tutorial, we consider the trotterized evolution of the transverse-"
"field Ising model on a 1D chain with nearest-neighbor interactions:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"    H = \\sum_{\\langle i,j\\rangle}J_{ij}Z_iZ_j + \\sum_i h_i X_i\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To implement the time evolution $e^{-iH\\tau}$, we divide the time interval $\\tau$ into $r$ steps and define $\\Delta\\tau=\\tau/r$. The second-order Trotter-Suzuki decomposition gives the following:\n"
"$$\n"
"    e^{-iH\\Delta\\tau}\\approx \\prod_i e^{-ih_i X_i\\Delta\\tau/2 } \\prod_{\\langle i,j\\rangle} e^{-iJ_{ij}Z_iZ_j\\Delta\\tau} \\prod_i e^{-ih_iX_i\\Delta\\tau/2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For circuit construction, each Trotter step is implemented as a sequence of "
"single-qubit $R_x$ rotations and two-qubit $R_{ZZ}$ gates. The circuit "
"begins by preparing the Néel state $|0101\\cdots\\rangle$ using X gates on "
"alternating qubits. Each subsequent Trotter step applies: (1) "
"$R_x(h_i\\Delta\\tau)$ on every qubit, (2) $R_{ZZ}(2J_{ij}\\Delta\\tau)$ on "
"each neighboring pair along the chain, and (3) $R_x(h_i\\Delta\\tau)$ again "
"on every qubit. The total circuit depth grows linearly with the number of "
"Trotter steps $r$.\n"
msgstr ""

